WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.520
Here is a perfectly smooth curve.

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:05.880
One over one plus x squared.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:10.780
It rises to a peak of one at zero and
fades away on both sides.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:14.980
It has no corners and no gaps, and it
never blows up.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:17.160
We will call it the bump.

00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:20.857
Near zero there is a simple recipe for it.

00:00:20.857 --> 00:00:24.234
One, minus x squared,
plus x to the fourth,

00:00:24.234 --> 00:00:26.807
minus x to the sixth, and so on.

00:00:26.807 --> 00:00:29.767
Each extra term should
make the recipe better.

00:00:30.367 --> 00:00:32.976
Between minus one and one, it works,

00:00:32.976 --> 00:00:36.527
though near the ends
it needs more and more terms.

00:00:36.527 --> 00:00:39.327
Just past one, it falls apart.

00:00:39.327 --> 00:00:44.847
At one point one, the recipe up to x to
the fortieth gives about twenty five.

00:00:44.847 --> 00:00:47.727
The bump itself is less
than one half there.

00:00:48.333 --> 00:00:51.093
Nothing special happens
to the curve at one.

00:00:51.093 --> 00:00:53.773
It is as smooth there as anywhere else.

00:00:53.773 --> 00:01:02.583
So what stops the recipe at exactly one?

00:01:02.583 --> 00:01:05.543
The answer is not on this line at all.

00:01:05.543 --> 00:01:10.683
Let x be a complex number, and the bump
has two points where it blows up.

00:01:10.683 --> 00:01:15.743
They sit at i and minus i, one unit above
and below zero.

00:01:15.743 --> 00:01:17.993
A recipe built at zero only works

00:01:17.993 --> 00:01:21.673
inside the largest circle around zero
that avoids them.

00:01:21.673 --> 00:01:23.783
That circle has radius one.

00:01:24.383 --> 00:01:29.088
So points we could not see from the real
line were deciding what happens on it.

00:01:29.088 --> 00:01:31.623
Complex functions are that rigid.

00:01:31.623 --> 00:01:35.297
By the end, the same idea will count
roots we never find

00:01:35.297 --> 00:01:38.503
and compute a real area
from points off the line.

00:01:38.503 --> 00:01:41.983
First, we need a way to see complex
functions at all.

00:01:45.783 --> 00:01:48.878
A complex number is a point in a plane.

00:01:48.878 --> 00:01:53.723
Its first part says how far right, its
second part how far up.

00:01:53.723 --> 00:01:58.583
We write it as x plus i y, and draw it as
an arrow from zero.

00:01:59.183 --> 00:02:03.063
Adding two complex numbers puts their
arrows tip to tail.

00:02:03.063 --> 00:02:05.863
Multiplying does something more
interesting.

00:02:05.863 --> 00:02:08.411
Multiply every point of the plane by i,

00:02:08.411 --> 00:02:11.743
and the whole plane turns
a quarter turn about zero.

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:15.495
So i times i is a half turn.

00:02:15.495 --> 00:02:17.755
It sends one to minus one.

00:02:17.755 --> 00:02:21.070
That is all that i squared
equals minus one means.

00:02:21.683 --> 00:02:25.603
Multiplying by any complex number does two
things at once.

00:02:25.603 --> 00:02:30.163
It stretches by that number's length and
turns by its angle.

00:02:30.163 --> 00:02:34.771
Multiply by one plus i, and the plane
grows by the square root of two

00:02:34.771 --> 00:02:36.803
and turns an eighth of a turn.

00:02:37.417 --> 00:02:41.957
Multiplying by one plus i once
makes an eighth of a turn.

00:02:41.957 --> 00:02:44.737
What happens if you multiply by it twice?

00:02:44.737 --> 00:02:53.433
Which number is one plus i, squared?

00:02:53.433 --> 00:02:57.266
A quarter turn, with the stretch
of the square root of two

00:02:57.266 --> 00:02:59.148
applied twice, which is two.

00:02:59.148 --> 00:03:02.803
So one plus i, squared, is two i.

00:03:02.803 --> 00:03:05.753
Lengths multiply and angles add.

00:03:05.753 --> 00:03:07.033
Hold on to that.

00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:09.135
Every complex derivative in this video

00:03:09.135 --> 00:03:11.513
will be one of these
stretch and turn moves.

00:03:15.317 --> 00:03:19.242
A complex function takes a point and
returns a point.

00:03:19.242 --> 00:03:21.817
Its graph would need four dimensions.

00:03:21.817 --> 00:03:24.857
Instead, draw two planes side by side.

00:03:24.857 --> 00:03:28.677
The input plane on the left, the output
plane on the right.

00:03:29.283 --> 00:03:30.983
Start with squaring.

00:03:30.983 --> 00:03:33.484
Lengths multiply and angles add,

00:03:33.484 --> 00:03:37.703
so squaring squares each length
and doubles each angle.

00:03:37.703 --> 00:03:41.443
Walk a point once around a circle of
radius one half.

00:03:41.443 --> 00:03:45.763
Its image goes around a circle of radius
one quarter, twice.

00:03:46.367 --> 00:03:48.767
Now a grid of straight lines.

00:03:48.767 --> 00:03:56.900
After squaring, will the vertical lines
stay straight?

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:01.324
No. Every vertical line off the axis
bends into a parabola,

00:04:01.324 --> 00:04:03.600
and so does every horizontal one.

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:06.520
The two axes fold in half instead.

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:09.608
The horizontal axis lands
on the positive real ray,

00:04:09.608 --> 00:04:12.140
and the vertical axis
on the negative one.

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:14.340
But look where the curves cross.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:18.820
They still meet at right angles
everywhere, except at zero.

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:20.140
Keep that in mind.

00:04:20.140 --> 00:04:22.740
It is the first sign of something rigid.

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:26.185
One over z does something else.

00:04:26.185 --> 00:04:30.910
Points near zero go far away, and far
points come close.

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:33.654
The unit circle lands on itself
as a whole,

00:04:33.654 --> 00:04:36.920
though each point flips
across the horizontal axis.

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:39.895
Inside and outside trade places.

00:04:39.895 --> 00:04:42.790
At zero itself there is no output at all.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:47.080
The exponential turns the grid into
something familiar.

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:51.240
Moving right by one multiplies
the output's length by e.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:56.400
Moving up turns the output, a full turn
for every two pi you climb.

00:04:56.400 --> 00:05:01.880
So vertical lines become circles, and
horizontal lines become rays from zero.

00:05:02.483 --> 00:05:06.523
Climb two pi, and you land
on exactly the same output.

00:05:06.523 --> 00:05:10.703
The exponential repeats as you climb, and
it never reaches zero.

00:05:10.703 --> 00:05:12.323
Both facts will come back.

00:05:16.133 --> 00:05:19.473
Two planes show only
a few curves at a time.

00:05:19.473 --> 00:05:23.438
To see a whole function at once,
paint the input plane.

00:05:23.438 --> 00:05:25.819
At each input point, find the output,

00:05:25.819 --> 00:05:28.778
and colour the point
by the output's direction.

00:05:28.778 --> 00:05:30.593
Pointing right is red.

00:05:30.593 --> 00:05:33.133
Pointing up is yellow green.

00:05:33.133 --> 00:05:36.773
Pointing left is teal, and pointing down
is violet.

00:05:37.383 --> 00:05:40.743
Colour alone hides how big the output is.

00:05:40.743 --> 00:05:43.263
So brightness climbs in bands.

00:05:43.263 --> 00:05:48.543
Each time the output's size grows by about
one point seven, a new band begins.

00:05:48.543 --> 00:05:52.663
Thin dark lines also mark every twelfth of
a turn in direction.

00:05:53.267 --> 00:05:54.842
Now square.

00:05:54.842 --> 00:05:59.767
Every colour appears twice around zero,
because squaring doubles each angle.

00:05:59.767 --> 00:06:04.227
And the colours run around zero
in the same order as the wheel itself.

00:06:04.833 --> 00:06:10.153
Around a point where the output is zero,
the colours always cycle in this order.

00:06:10.153 --> 00:06:15.403
Once for a simple zero, twice for a zero
of order two, and so on.

00:06:15.403 --> 00:06:19.633
That is how to spot a zero, and its order,
in a single glance.

00:06:27.733 --> 00:06:30.853
One over z runs the colours the other way.

00:06:30.853 --> 00:06:35.333
Around a pole, where the output blows up,
the colours cycle backwards.

00:06:35.933 --> 00:06:40.053
Here is the bump,
one over one plus z squared.

00:06:40.053 --> 00:06:43.233
Before the colours appear, where do you
expect its poles,

00:06:43.233 --> 00:06:52.267
and how many times will the colours turn
around each one?

00:06:52.267 --> 00:06:57.367
Two poles, at i and minus i, each with one
backwards cycle.

00:06:57.367 --> 00:07:01.822
And the circle of radius one around zero
just touches them.

00:07:01.822 --> 00:07:04.827
That is the opening puzzle,
seen in colour.

00:07:09.033 --> 00:07:10.873
Polynomials first.

00:07:10.873 --> 00:07:16.313
z cubed minus one has three zeros, evenly
spaced on the unit circle.

00:07:16.313 --> 00:07:19.716
Far from zero, z cubed
swamps the minus one,

00:07:19.716 --> 00:07:23.673
so a large loop sees
the colours cycle three times.

00:07:23.673 --> 00:07:25.433
Three zeros inside.

00:07:26.033 --> 00:07:29.633
Rational functions divide one polynomial
by another.

00:07:29.633 --> 00:07:33.753
Here z minus one, over z plus one squared.

00:07:33.753 --> 00:07:37.886
A simple zero at one,
and at minus one a pole of order two,

00:07:37.886 --> 00:07:40.593
where the colours run backwards twice.

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:45.380
The exponential has no zeros and no poles
anywhere.

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:50.820
Its direction depends only on height, and
its size only on how far right you are.

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:55.680
So its tiles are exact squares, repeating
every two pi as you climb.

00:07:56.283 --> 00:08:01.723
Sine has zeros at every multiple of pi on
the real line, and nowhere else.

00:08:01.723 --> 00:08:04.063
On the real line it never passes one.

00:08:04.063 --> 00:08:06.898
But off the line it grows fast.

00:08:06.898 --> 00:08:12.603
Sine of i is about one point one eight i,
and sine of three i is about ten i.

00:08:13.217 --> 00:08:15.917
Tangent is sine over cosine.

00:08:15.917 --> 00:08:19.377
Its zeros and poles alternate
along the real line.

00:08:19.377 --> 00:08:22.655
Far above the line, tangent
settles toward i,

00:08:22.655 --> 00:08:26.977
and far below toward minus i,
so the colours there go flat.

00:08:27.583 --> 00:08:30.643
The gamma function extends the factorial.

00:08:30.643 --> 00:08:33.983
Gamma of n plus one is n factorial.

00:08:33.983 --> 00:08:38.983
It starts as an integral that only makes
sense when the real part of z is positive.

00:08:38.983 --> 00:08:44.026
Yet the picture continues left,
with a pole at zero, at minus one,

00:08:44.026 --> 00:08:47.983
at minus two, and on forever,
and no zeros anywhere.

00:08:47.983 --> 00:08:50.463
Chapter fourteen shows how it gets there.

00:08:51.067 --> 00:08:55.537
The zeta function starts as one,
plus one over two to the s,

00:08:55.537 --> 00:08:58.492
plus one over three to the s,
and so on.

00:08:58.492 --> 00:09:03.027
That sum only settles when the real part
of s is bigger than one.

00:09:03.027 --> 00:09:04.387
The picture goes further.

00:09:04.387 --> 00:09:06.767
A single pole at one.

00:09:06.767 --> 00:09:10.027
Zeros at minus two, minus four, and on.

00:09:10.027 --> 00:09:14.187
And more zeros climbing the line
where the real part is one half.

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:18.954
Apart from those at minus two,
minus four, and on,

00:09:18.954 --> 00:09:22.260
no zero found so far
sits off that line.

00:09:22.260 --> 00:09:27.320
Whether all the rest lie on it
is the Riemann hypothesis, still unsolved.

00:09:27.320 --> 00:09:29.200
A picture cannot settle it.

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:35.680
These pictures share a pattern.

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:38.780
Zeros and poles are isolated points.

00:09:38.780 --> 00:09:43.780
Between them, the colours flow smoothly in
little tiles that are nearly square.

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:45.940
That is not a coincidence.

00:09:45.940 --> 00:09:48.980
It comes from what
a complex derivative demands.

00:09:53.683 --> 00:09:56.809
For a real function,
the derivative compares

00:09:56.809 --> 00:09:59.788
a small change out
with a small change in.

00:09:59.788 --> 00:10:01.993
Do the same for a complex function.

00:10:01.993 --> 00:10:08.323
Step from z by a small complex number h,
and divide the change in the output by h.

00:10:08.323 --> 00:10:11.446
As h shrinks to zero,
that ratio should settle

00:10:11.446 --> 00:10:14.083
on one complex number, the derivative.

00:10:14.683 --> 00:10:17.963
In the plane, h can shrink from any
direction.

00:10:17.963 --> 00:10:21.623
From the right, from above,
along a spiral.

00:10:21.623 --> 00:10:26.713
The derivative exists only if every one of
those approaches gives the same answer.

00:10:26.713 --> 00:10:29.403
That demand is much stronger
than it looks.

00:10:30.017 --> 00:10:31.097
Here is why.

00:10:31.097 --> 00:10:34.600
If the ratio is the same number
from every direction,

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:38.777
then near z, the function moves
every small arrow the same way.

00:10:38.777 --> 00:10:41.157
It multiplies it by the derivative.

00:10:41.157 --> 00:10:45.177
And multiplying by a complex number
is a stretch and a turn.

00:10:45.177 --> 00:10:49.177
So a tiny square around z lands
as a tiny square,

00:10:49.177 --> 00:10:52.177
bigger and turned, but still square.

00:10:52.783 --> 00:10:57.437
Squaring at one plus i has derivative
two times one plus i,

00:10:57.437 --> 00:10:59.363
which is two plus two i.

00:10:59.363 --> 00:11:03.663
It stretches by about two point eight and
turns an eighth of a turn.

00:11:03.663 --> 00:11:07.903
Shrink the square, and its image gets ever
closer to exactly that.

00:11:08.517 --> 00:11:10.942
Now write the rule in coordinates.

00:11:10.942 --> 00:11:13.697
Call the output's parts u and v.

00:11:13.697 --> 00:11:19.557
Step to the right, and the ratio becomes
how fast u and v change as x changes.

00:11:19.557 --> 00:11:24.051
Step up, by i times a small number,
and you also divide by i,

00:11:24.051 --> 00:11:27.197
which turns the answer back
a quarter turn.

00:11:27.197 --> 00:11:29.397
The two answers must agree.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:31.820
That gives two equations.

00:11:31.820 --> 00:11:36.160
The rate of u along x
equals the rate of v along y.

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:40.720
The rate of v along x is minus the rate of
u along y.

00:11:40.720 --> 00:11:43.360
These are the Cauchy Riemann equations.

00:11:43.967 --> 00:11:48.377
In matrix form, the local map
must be a stretch and a turn,

00:11:48.377 --> 00:11:50.887
with no squash and no reflection.

00:11:50.887 --> 00:11:57.352
For squaring at one plus i, the four rates
are two, minus two, two, and two.

00:11:57.352 --> 00:12:01.327
That grid is exactly multiplication
by two plus two i.

00:12:01.933 --> 00:12:03.933
Here is a simple map.

00:12:03.933 --> 00:12:07.233
Reflect the plane
across the horizontal axis.

00:12:07.233 --> 00:12:11.373
It sends x plus i y to x minus i y.

00:12:11.373 --> 00:12:19.383
Does it have a complex derivative
anywhere?

00:12:19.383 --> 00:12:20.583
Nowhere.

00:12:20.583 --> 00:12:23.303
Step to the right, and the ratio is one.

00:12:23.303 --> 00:12:26.443
Step up, and the ratio is minus one.

00:12:26.443 --> 00:12:31.623
A reflection reverses every little square,
and no stretch and turn can do that.

00:12:31.623 --> 00:12:33.503
Squashing fails too.

00:12:33.503 --> 00:12:37.143
Double every height, and squares become
tall rectangles.

00:12:40.850 --> 00:12:44.410
Where the derivative is zero,
the picture changes.

00:12:44.410 --> 00:12:47.690
Squaring has derivative zero at zero.

00:12:47.690 --> 00:12:50.264
Two lines crossing there
at a right angle

00:12:50.264 --> 00:12:53.610
come out as two rays
pointing in opposite directions.

00:12:53.610 --> 00:12:57.570
The angle has doubled, from a right angle
to a straight line.

00:12:58.183 --> 00:13:01.563
So a nonzero derivative keeps angles.

00:13:01.563 --> 00:13:04.423
A map like that is called conformal.

00:13:04.423 --> 00:13:08.089
A point where the derivative is zero
is a critical point,

00:13:08.089 --> 00:13:10.183
and there angles get multiplied.

00:13:15.283 --> 00:13:17.208
A subtler question.

00:13:17.208 --> 00:13:21.063
Take the function that sends
z to the square of its length.

00:13:21.063 --> 00:13:29.783
At zero, does it have a complex
derivative?

00:13:29.783 --> 00:13:31.063
It does.

00:13:31.063 --> 00:13:35.468
The ratio is the square of the length of
h, divided by h.

00:13:35.468 --> 00:13:40.353
Its size is the length of h, which shrinks
to zero from every direction.

00:13:40.353 --> 00:13:44.023
So the derivative at zero exists,
and it is zero.

00:13:44.633 --> 00:13:46.173
Move to the point one.

00:13:46.173 --> 00:13:49.113
Step right, and the ratio heads to two.

00:13:49.113 --> 00:13:52.063
Step up, and it heads to zero.

00:13:52.063 --> 00:13:57.071
This function has a complex derivative
at exactly one point in the whole plane,

00:13:57.071 --> 00:13:58.933
and that buys almost nothing.

00:13:58.933 --> 00:14:03.084
Multiply it by any bounded function
at all, smooth or jagged,

00:14:03.084 --> 00:14:06.473
and the product still has
derivative zero at zero.

00:14:07.083 --> 00:14:09.883
The strong results ahead need more.

00:14:09.883 --> 00:14:14.014
They need the derivative to exist
at every point of an open region,

00:14:14.014 --> 00:14:18.583
so that around each point there is
a small disk where it works everywhere.

00:14:18.583 --> 00:14:22.238
A function like that is called holomorphic
on the region.

00:14:22.238 --> 00:14:25.323
From here on, every claim carries that
condition.

00:14:29.033 --> 00:14:33.033
To see what holomorphic buys, we need one
new tool.

00:14:33.033 --> 00:14:35.713
Walk around a loop in small steps.

00:14:35.713 --> 00:14:38.617
At each step, take
the function's value there,

00:14:38.617 --> 00:14:41.713
and multiply it by the step,
as a complex number.

00:14:41.713 --> 00:14:44.153
Then add all those products.

00:14:44.153 --> 00:14:48.553
As the steps shrink, the total is
the contour integral around the loop.

00:14:49.167 --> 00:14:53.767
Draw each product as an arrow,
and lay the arrows tip to tail.

00:14:53.767 --> 00:14:57.098
For f of z equals z,
around the unit circle,

00:14:57.098 --> 00:14:59.887
the arrows curl around and close up.

00:14:59.887 --> 00:15:01.407
The total is zero.

00:15:02.017 --> 00:15:03.697
Now one over z.

00:15:03.697 --> 00:15:08.343
At each step, dividing by z undoes
the turn of the step's position,

00:15:08.343 --> 00:15:10.877
and what is left points straight up.

00:15:10.877 --> 00:15:13.157
The arrows stack into a column.

00:15:13.157 --> 00:15:18.817
As the steps shrink, the total becomes
two pi i, whatever the size of the circle.

00:15:19.417 --> 00:15:22.737
Every other whole power of z gives zero.

00:15:22.737 --> 00:15:25.177
The arrows always close up.

00:15:25.177 --> 00:15:30.297
Only one over z survives, and that single
fact powers most of what follows.

00:15:30.900 --> 00:15:33.180
Cauchy's theorem says more.

00:15:33.180 --> 00:15:37.540
If a function is holomorphic
on a loop and everywhere inside it,

00:15:37.540 --> 00:15:39.340
its loop integral is zero.

00:15:39.340 --> 00:15:43.017
We will prove the pieces we need,
starting with a square,

00:15:43.017 --> 00:15:47.220
and a function holomorphic on it,
inside it, and a little beyond.

00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:52.320
Cut the square into four smaller squares,
and walk each one counterclockwise.

00:15:52.320 --> 00:15:56.709
When we add their four loop integrals,
every shared edge is walked

00:15:56.709 --> 00:16:00.760
twice, in opposite directions, so those
parts cancel exactly.

00:16:00.760 --> 00:16:04.900
What remains is the loop integral around
the original square.

00:16:04.900 --> 00:16:07.760
Now suppose that integral were not zero.

00:16:07.760 --> 00:16:10.489
Then some small square must have
an integral

00:16:10.489 --> 00:16:12.520
at least a quarter of that size.

00:16:12.520 --> 00:16:16.860
Four integrals each under a quarter of
that size could not add up to it.

00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:19.860
Keep that square,
and cut it into four again.

00:16:19.860 --> 00:16:24.900
Each time, keep a piece whose integral has
at least a quarter of its parent's size.

00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:29.640
These nested squares shrink
toward a single point, which we call c.

00:16:29.640 --> 00:16:33.820
From here on, we use the derivative at
that one point only.

00:16:33.820 --> 00:16:37.616
Near c, the function is a constant,
plus a linear term

00:16:37.616 --> 00:16:41.340
that stretches and turns z minus c,
plus a remainder.

00:16:41.340 --> 00:16:43.318
The constant integrates to zero,

00:16:43.318 --> 00:16:46.780
because the steps around a closed loop
add up to nothing.

00:16:46.780 --> 00:16:49.171
The linear term integrates to zero too,

00:16:49.171 --> 00:16:51.806
because it is the derivative
of a quadratic,

00:16:51.806 --> 00:16:55.300
whose changes around a closed loop
also add up to nothing.

00:16:55.300 --> 00:16:57.800
So only the remainder contributes.

00:16:57.800 --> 00:17:00.780
Pick any tiny factor, and call it epsilon.

00:17:00.780 --> 00:17:06.777
Close enough to c, the remainder's size is
at most epsilon times the distance to c,

00:17:06.777 --> 00:17:10.800
and that is exactly what
having a derivative at c means.

00:17:10.800 --> 00:17:15.120
After enough cuts, the whole nested square
lies that close.

00:17:15.120 --> 00:17:18.624
There, the distance to c is at most
the square's diagonal,

00:17:18.624 --> 00:17:20.960
and the steps add up to the perimeter.

00:17:20.960 --> 00:17:24.327
So the integral around that square has
size at most

00:17:24.327 --> 00:17:27.560
epsilon, times its diagonal, times its
perimeter.

00:17:27.560 --> 00:17:31.064
Each cut halves the diagonal
and halves the perimeter,

00:17:31.064 --> 00:17:34.040
so this bound shrinks
to a quarter every time.

00:17:34.040 --> 00:17:38.240
But our rule kept at least a quarter of
the integral's size at every cut.

00:17:38.240 --> 00:17:41.781
So after n cuts, the original integral's
size is at most

00:17:41.781 --> 00:17:45.580
four to the n times the size of the small
square's integral.

00:17:45.580 --> 00:17:48.787
The small square's integral
has size at most epsilon,

00:17:48.787 --> 00:17:52.920
times the original diagonal and perimeter,
divided by four to the n.

00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:55.559
Multiplying by four to the n
cancels the fours,

00:17:55.559 --> 00:17:57.969
so the original integral's size is at most

00:17:57.969 --> 00:18:00.780
epsilon times the original diagonal
and perimeter.

00:18:00.780 --> 00:18:06.000
Since epsilon can be as small as we like,
the integral is zero.

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:08.840
The same shrinking argument
works for a triangle.

00:18:08.840 --> 00:18:13.780
Join the midpoints of its sides, and you
get four triangles at half the size.

00:18:13.780 --> 00:18:18.165
Walk all four counterclockwise,
even the upside down one in the middle,

00:18:18.165 --> 00:18:20.420
and their shared edges cancel again.

00:18:20.420 --> 00:18:23.152
The longest side plays
the diagonal's part,

00:18:23.152 --> 00:18:26.080
and it halves with the perimeter
at every cut.

00:18:26.080 --> 00:18:29.540
So around every triangle, the integral is
zero too.

00:18:38.350 --> 00:18:40.450
Here is the payoff.

00:18:40.450 --> 00:18:44.540
Take a function holomorphic on a disk and
a little beyond

00:18:44.540 --> 00:18:48.850
its edge, and divide it by z minus a,
where a is the centre.

00:18:48.850 --> 00:18:51.310
Split that quotient into two pieces.

00:18:51.310 --> 00:18:54.859
The first is f of a,
times one over z minus a,

00:18:54.859 --> 00:18:59.590
and we already know its loop integral:
two pi i times f of a.

00:18:59.590 --> 00:19:06.130
The second is the difference quotient: f
of z minus f of a, over z minus a.

00:19:06.130 --> 00:19:09.726
It is undefined at a itself,
so it has a hole in the middle,

00:19:09.726 --> 00:19:12.530
and we cannot simply call it
holomorphic there.

00:19:12.530 --> 00:19:17.079
But as z approaches a,
it approaches the derivative at a,

00:19:17.079 --> 00:19:20.490
so near a its size stays below some bound.

00:19:20.490 --> 00:19:23.410
Draw a small circle around the hole.

00:19:23.410 --> 00:19:27.450
Between the two circles, the difference
quotient is holomorphic.

00:19:27.450 --> 00:19:31.467
Trace each circle with a polygon,
and tile the ring between them

00:19:31.467 --> 00:19:33.890
with triangles,
keeping the hole empty.

00:19:33.890 --> 00:19:37.541
We saw that the shrinking argument
works for triangles:

00:19:37.541 --> 00:19:40.110
around each one, the integral is zero.

00:19:40.110 --> 00:19:42.690
Now add up all the triangles.

00:19:42.690 --> 00:19:46.615
Every inside edge is walked twice,
in opposite directions,

00:19:46.615 --> 00:19:48.130
so those parts cancel.

00:19:48.130 --> 00:19:50.494
What survives is the outer polygon,

00:19:50.494 --> 00:19:53.870
counterclockwise, and the inner polygon,
clockwise.

00:19:53.870 --> 00:19:59.190
So the two polygons, both walked
counterclockwise, have the same integral.

00:19:59.190 --> 00:20:04.098
Now keep the small circle fixed,
and give the polygons more and more sides,

00:20:04.098 --> 00:20:05.690
so they hug the circles.

00:20:05.690 --> 00:20:09.075
Each short chord and its arc
share their endpoints,

00:20:09.075 --> 00:20:12.190
so a constant has the same integral
along both.

00:20:12.190 --> 00:20:16.426
The difference comes only from how much
the quotient wobbles there,

00:20:16.426 --> 00:20:20.470
and its size is at most that wobble times
their combined length.

00:20:20.470 --> 00:20:23.372
Continuity makes every wobble
small at once,

00:20:23.372 --> 00:20:25.870
while the total length stays bounded.

00:20:25.870 --> 00:20:30.525
So in the limit, the big circle's integral
equals the small circle's,

00:20:30.525 --> 00:20:32.510
both walked counterclockwise.

00:20:32.510 --> 00:20:35.290
That holds for every small radius.

00:20:35.290 --> 00:20:38.010
Only now do we shrink the small circle.

00:20:38.010 --> 00:20:41.994
The magnitude of its integral
is at most its circumference,

00:20:41.994 --> 00:20:46.390
two pi times its radius, times that bound,
and that goes to zero.

00:20:46.390 --> 00:20:50.610
But the big circle's integral never
changed, so it must be zero.

00:20:50.610 --> 00:20:53.026
The second piece contributes nothing,

00:20:53.026 --> 00:20:56.030
and the loop integral is
two pi i times f of a.

00:20:56.633 --> 00:21:00.493
Around a circle centred at a,
that has a simple meaning.

00:21:00.493 --> 00:21:04.893
The value at the centre is the average of
the values on the circle.

00:21:04.893 --> 00:21:07.113
This is Cauchy's integral formula.

00:21:07.717 --> 00:21:12.477
Try it on the bump, on a circle of radius
one half around zero.

00:21:12.477 --> 00:21:16.536
Eight sample points already average
to within half a percent of one,

00:21:16.536 --> 00:21:18.597
which is the bump's value at zero.

00:21:18.597 --> 00:21:22.437
With sixteen samples, it is off by about
fifteen millionths.

00:21:23.050 --> 00:21:25.770
Now take a circle of radius two.

00:21:25.770 --> 00:21:28.370
The bump is still one at the centre.

00:21:28.370 --> 00:21:37.300
Is the average around this bigger circle
still one?

00:21:37.300 --> 00:21:38.780
It is zero.

00:21:38.780 --> 00:21:42.613
The bigger circle has swallowed
the poles at i and minus i,

00:21:42.613 --> 00:21:44.860
where the bump is not holomorphic.

00:21:44.860 --> 00:21:48.380
Each pole pulls the average
down by one half.

00:21:48.380 --> 00:21:51.756
For this formula, a sufficient condition
is that the function

00:21:51.756 --> 00:21:55.300
is holomorphic throughout the disk
and a little beyond its edge.

00:21:58.900 --> 00:22:02.220
The same circle tells you
more than the value.

00:22:02.220 --> 00:22:07.400
Turn each sample back by its own angle,
average, and divide by the radius.

00:22:07.400 --> 00:22:09.740
That gives the slope at the centre.

00:22:09.740 --> 00:22:13.503
Turn back by twice the angle
and divide by the radius squared,

00:22:13.503 --> 00:22:16.340
and you get the next coefficient
of the recipe.

00:22:16.340 --> 00:22:19.860
Every derivative at the centre is hidden
in the boundary values.

00:22:20.467 --> 00:22:25.300
Those coefficients build a power series,
and Cauchy's formula shows it converges

00:22:25.300 --> 00:22:29.827
on the largest disk around the centre
where the function stays holomorphic.

00:22:29.827 --> 00:22:31.667
That answers the opening puzzle.

00:22:31.667 --> 00:22:36.387
The bump is holomorphic everywhere except
at i and minus i.

00:22:36.387 --> 00:22:40.126
From zero they are one unit away,
so the recipe works out

00:22:40.126 --> 00:22:44.067
to radius one, and since the bump blows up
at i, no further.

00:22:44.667 --> 00:22:47.707
Recentre the recipe at x equals one.

00:22:47.707 --> 00:22:57.367
How far along the real line
will it work now?

00:22:57.367 --> 00:23:00.367
The nearest poles are now the square root
of two away.

00:23:00.367 --> 00:23:04.384
So the new recipe works
from about minus point four one

00:23:04.384 --> 00:23:06.467
to about two point four one.

00:23:06.467 --> 00:23:09.207
A computer check of sixty terms agrees.

00:23:13.417 --> 00:23:17.137
This makes holomorphic functions
astonishingly rigid.

00:23:17.137 --> 00:23:19.697
Take two of them on a connected region.

00:23:19.697 --> 00:23:23.777
If they agree along any little curve, they
agree everywhere.

00:23:23.777 --> 00:23:25.922
Even agreeing on a sequence of points

00:23:25.922 --> 00:23:28.937
that piles up at a point
inside the region is enough.

00:23:29.550 --> 00:23:34.710
Sine squared plus cosine squared equals
one for every real number.

00:23:34.710 --> 00:23:39.690
Both sides are holomorphic on the whole
plane, and they agree along the real line.

00:23:39.690 --> 00:23:42.876
So the identity holds
for every complex number,

00:23:42.876 --> 00:23:45.370
even where sine and cosine are huge.

00:23:45.370 --> 00:23:48.744
At three i, sine squared
is about minus one hundred,

00:23:48.744 --> 00:23:51.590
cosine squared is about
one hundred and one,

00:23:51.590 --> 00:23:53.310
and they still add to one.

00:23:53.917 --> 00:23:57.317
Real smooth functions have no such
rigidity.

00:23:57.317 --> 00:24:02.597
This curve is exactly zero for every
negative x, then rises smoothly.

00:24:02.597 --> 00:24:07.357
It agrees with zero on a whole half line,
yet it is not zero.

00:24:07.357 --> 00:24:11.092
Every derivative exists at zero,
and every one is zero,

00:24:11.092 --> 00:24:13.997
so its power series misses the whole rise.

00:24:14.600 --> 00:24:18.071
Sine of one over z
is zero at one over pi,

00:24:18.071 --> 00:24:21.880
one over two pi, one over three pi,
and so on.

00:24:21.880 --> 00:24:24.360
Those zeros pile up at zero.

00:24:24.360 --> 00:24:32.700
Must the function be zero everywhere?

00:24:32.700 --> 00:24:37.310
No. The pile up point is zero,
and zero is not in the region,

00:24:37.310 --> 00:24:40.020
because the function is undefined there.

00:24:40.020 --> 00:24:43.440
The theorem needs the pile up point inside
the region.

00:24:43.440 --> 00:24:47.340
Nothing forces sine of one over z to
vanish, and it doesn't.

00:24:56.450 --> 00:24:59.450
Rigidity also limits what is possible.

00:24:59.450 --> 00:25:05.050
A function holomorphic on the whole plane
that stays bounded must be constant.

00:25:05.050 --> 00:25:06.910
That is Liouville's theorem.

00:25:06.910 --> 00:25:11.269
It is why sine, trapped between
minus one and one on the real line,

00:25:11.269 --> 00:25:13.250
must grow huge somewhere else.

00:25:17.450 --> 00:25:18.510
Back to colour.

00:25:18.510 --> 00:25:22.810
Walk a loop in the input plane and watch
the output's direction.

00:25:22.810 --> 00:25:28.330
Around a simple zero, the output makes one
full turn, the same way you walk.

00:25:28.330 --> 00:25:31.290
Around a zero of order two, it makes two.

00:25:31.290 --> 00:25:35.050
Around a simple pole, it makes one turn
the other way.

00:25:36.467 --> 00:25:39.967
For a loop around several of them,
the turns add.

00:25:39.967 --> 00:25:45.167
So the number of turns equals
the zeros inside minus the poles inside,

00:25:45.167 --> 00:25:47.127
each counted by its order.

00:25:47.127 --> 00:25:49.267
This is the argument principle.

00:25:49.267 --> 00:25:52.542
It needs a loop that winds once
counterclockwise,

00:25:52.542 --> 00:25:54.657
passes through no zero or pole,

00:25:54.657 --> 00:25:58.547
and a function that is holomorphic
inside except at poles.

00:25:59.150 --> 00:26:02.956
Here is a function with a zero
at zero point four five

00:26:02.956 --> 00:26:05.470
and a pole at one point three five.

00:26:05.470 --> 00:26:09.806
A circle of radius zero point eight
encloses only the zero,

00:26:09.806 --> 00:26:11.750
and the output turns once.

00:26:11.750 --> 00:26:15.470
Grow the circle to one point seven, and it
encloses both.

00:26:15.470 --> 00:26:20.150
The zero's turn and the pole's turn
cancel, so the count is zero.

00:26:20.150 --> 00:26:22.790
The count is always a net total.

00:26:22.790 --> 00:26:27.710
An output could circle zero once and then
unwind, and it would still count zero.

00:26:28.317 --> 00:26:31.806
At radius one point three five exactly,
the loop runs

00:26:31.806 --> 00:26:35.497
through the pole, and the output shoots
off to infinity.

00:26:35.497 --> 00:26:38.077
There the count has no meaning.

00:26:38.077 --> 00:26:40.877
The loop must avoid every zero and pole.

00:26:41.483 --> 00:26:46.492
This polynomial,
z to the fifth minus three z plus one,

00:26:46.492 --> 00:26:47.883
has five roots.

00:26:47.883 --> 00:26:57.617
How many of them lie
inside the unit circle?

00:26:57.617 --> 00:27:03.617
One. Walk the unit circle, and the output
turns around zero exactly once.

00:27:03.617 --> 00:27:08.359
On that circle, the minus three z term is
always bigger than the other

00:27:08.359 --> 00:27:13.377
two terms combined, so the output follows
minus three z, which turns once.

00:27:20.983 --> 00:27:26.223
The same trick proves that every
nonconstant complex polynomial has roots.

00:27:26.223 --> 00:27:30.703
Divide by its leading coefficient, which
leaves its roots unchanged.

00:27:30.703 --> 00:27:35.103
Now it starts with z to the n, for some
positive integer n.

00:27:35.103 --> 00:27:39.643
On a huge circle, z to the n
dwarfs the other terms.

00:27:39.643 --> 00:27:43.510
Picture z to the n as a walker
going n times around zero,

00:27:43.510 --> 00:27:46.203
and the polynomial as a dog on a leash.

00:27:46.203 --> 00:27:49.893
The leash is shorter than
the walker's distance from zero,

00:27:49.893 --> 00:27:52.223
so the dog circles zero n times too.

00:27:52.223 --> 00:27:56.423
So there are exactly n roots inside,
counted by order.

00:27:57.033 --> 00:27:58.553
For programmers.

00:27:58.553 --> 00:28:02.233
This counts roots inside a loop without
finding them.

00:28:02.233 --> 00:28:06.540
Walk the loop in small steps,
add up the change in the output's angle

00:28:06.540 --> 00:28:09.073
at each step, and divide by a full turn.

00:28:09.073 --> 00:28:12.833
But samples alone can miss a turn made
quickly between them.

00:28:12.833 --> 00:28:15.636
So bound how fast f can change
on the loop,

00:28:15.636 --> 00:28:18.972
and take steps so small
that the output cannot move

00:28:18.972 --> 00:28:21.173
as far as its distance from zero.

00:28:21.173 --> 00:28:25.113
Then no turn can hide between samples, and
the count is certain.

00:28:36.317 --> 00:28:39.837
Counting turns is one use
of a bigger idea.

00:28:39.837 --> 00:28:44.839
Near a pole, a function looks like
a few negative powers of z minus p,

00:28:44.839 --> 00:28:46.797
plus something holomorphic.

00:28:46.797 --> 00:28:51.247
Around a small loop,
all of those terms integrate to zero,

00:28:51.247 --> 00:28:53.277
except one over z minus p.

00:28:53.277 --> 00:28:55.437
Its coefficient is called the residue.

00:28:56.050 --> 00:29:01.550
So a loop integral is two pi i times
the sum of the residues inside.

00:29:01.550 --> 00:29:03.610
That is the residue theorem.

00:29:03.610 --> 00:29:08.820
For a simple pole of g over h,
where h is zero but its derivative is not,

00:29:08.820 --> 00:29:13.090
the residue is g over the derivative of h,
both at the pole.

00:29:13.700 --> 00:29:16.580
The turns we counted are residues too.

00:29:16.580 --> 00:29:21.380
Near a zero of order m, f prime over f has
residue m.

00:29:21.380 --> 00:29:25.140
Near a pole of order n,
its residue is minus n.

00:29:25.750 --> 00:29:29.368
For the bump, the bottom
is one plus z squared,

00:29:29.368 --> 00:29:31.570
and its derivative is two z.

00:29:31.570 --> 00:29:34.410
At i, that is two i.

00:29:34.410 --> 00:29:37.830
So the residue of the bump at i
is one over two i.

00:29:42.033 --> 00:29:43.553
Now a real question.

00:29:43.553 --> 00:29:48.193
What is the area under the bump,
along the whole real line?

00:29:48.193 --> 00:29:49.753
You may know it from calculus.

00:29:49.753 --> 00:29:54.873
The antiderivative is the arctangent, and
the area is pi.

00:29:54.873 --> 00:29:58.033
Here is a second way, with no
antiderivative at all.

00:29:58.633 --> 00:30:01.908
Walk along the real line
from minus R to R,

00:30:01.908 --> 00:30:05.573
then come back along
a big half circle above it.

00:30:05.573 --> 00:30:08.573
Inside this loop sits one pole, at i.

00:30:08.573 --> 00:30:13.213
So the loop integral is
two pi i, times one over two i.

00:30:13.213 --> 00:30:15.273
That is exactly pi.

00:30:15.883 --> 00:30:19.438
On the half circle,
z squared has length R squared,

00:30:19.438 --> 00:30:23.063
so the bump is at most
one over R squared minus one.

00:30:23.063 --> 00:30:25.783
The half circle has length pi R.

00:30:25.783 --> 00:30:30.479
So that part of the loop is at most
pi R over R squared minus one,

00:30:30.479 --> 00:30:32.863
which shrinks to zero as R grows.

00:30:32.863 --> 00:30:35.403
All of pi comes from the real line.

00:30:36.017 --> 00:30:38.097
Now a harder area.

00:30:38.097 --> 00:30:41.117
One over one plus x to the fourth.

00:30:41.117 --> 00:30:44.657
Its antiderivative exists, but it looks
like this.

00:30:44.657 --> 00:30:47.057
Instead, use the same loop.

00:30:47.667 --> 00:30:51.827
The bottom is zero at the four fourth
roots of minus one.

00:30:51.827 --> 00:30:56.141
They sit on the unit circle
at forty five, one hundred thirty five,

00:30:56.141 --> 00:30:59.867
two hundred twenty five
and three hundred fifteen degrees.

00:30:59.867 --> 00:31:08.800
Which ones lie inside
the half circle loop?

00:31:08.800 --> 00:31:11.040
The two above the real line.

00:31:11.040 --> 00:31:13.520
Call them p one and p two.

00:31:14.133 --> 00:31:15.867
Each is a simple pole,

00:31:15.867 --> 00:31:22.013
so its residue is one over the derivative
of the bottom, one over four p cubed.

00:31:22.013 --> 00:31:26.793
Because p to the fourth is minus one,
one over p cubed is minus p.

00:31:26.793 --> 00:31:30.053
So each residue is minus p over four.

00:31:30.667 --> 00:31:32.067
Add the two.

00:31:32.067 --> 00:31:34.805
The real parts of p one and p two cancel,

00:31:34.805 --> 00:31:38.747
and each has imaginary part
the square root of two over two.

00:31:38.747 --> 00:31:44.027
So the residues add to minus i times
the square root of two, over four.

00:31:44.633 --> 00:31:47.213
Multiply by two pi i.

00:31:47.213 --> 00:31:51.210
The two i's make minus one,
which cancels the minus sign,

00:31:51.210 --> 00:31:54.493
and the area is
pi over the square root of two.

00:31:54.493 --> 00:31:56.313
About two point two two.

00:31:56.917 --> 00:31:58.597
And the half circle?

00:31:58.597 --> 00:32:03.335
On it, the function is at most
one over R to the fourth minus one,

00:32:03.335 --> 00:32:07.637
so that part is at most
pi R over R to the fourth minus one.

00:32:07.637 --> 00:32:11.857
At R equals ten, that is already less than
one three hundredth.

00:32:11.857 --> 00:32:16.597
A numerical sum along the real line agrees
with pi over the square root of two.

00:32:17.200 --> 00:32:18.840
One honest note.

00:32:18.840 --> 00:32:21.692
The real integral converges on its own,

00:32:21.692 --> 00:32:25.640
because the function falls off
fast in both directions.

00:32:25.640 --> 00:32:30.080
That is what lets us find it as the limit
of the stretch from minus R to R.

00:32:39.383 --> 00:32:42.503
The bump's recipe stops at radius one.

00:32:42.503 --> 00:32:45.463
Is the bump itself stuck
inside that circle?

00:32:45.463 --> 00:32:46.623
Of course not.

00:32:46.623 --> 00:32:50.323
We know it everywhere
except at i and minus i.

00:32:50.323 --> 00:32:55.763
A function given by a series can often be
extended beyond where its series works.

00:32:55.763 --> 00:32:58.183
This is called analytic continuation.

00:32:58.783 --> 00:33:04.103
The clearest example is
one, plus z, plus z squared, and so on.

00:33:04.103 --> 00:33:08.603
Inside the unit circle it adds up
to one over one minus z.

00:33:08.603 --> 00:33:11.543
At one half, the sums settle on two.

00:33:11.543 --> 00:33:14.423
At one point two, they grow without limit.

00:33:14.423 --> 00:33:18.563
But one over one minus z makes perfect
sense at one point two.

00:33:18.563 --> 00:33:20.223
It equals minus five.

00:33:20.833 --> 00:33:23.606
Because holomorphic functions are rigid,

00:33:23.606 --> 00:33:28.873
a function has at most one holomorphic
extension to a given connected region.

00:33:28.873 --> 00:33:33.913
Two extensions that agree on the small
disk agree everywhere in that region.

00:33:33.913 --> 00:33:37.090
You can also build
an extension step by step.

00:33:37.090 --> 00:33:40.134
Recentre the series
at a point inside the disk,

00:33:40.134 --> 00:33:42.913
and its new disk pokes
outside the old one.

00:33:42.913 --> 00:33:47.953
Chain disks along a path, and you can walk
all the way around the pole at one.

00:33:47.953 --> 00:33:50.433
Each step along the path is forced.

00:33:50.433 --> 00:33:53.987
Here every path gives back
one over one minus z,

00:33:53.987 --> 00:33:55.953
but that is not always so.

00:34:02.067 --> 00:34:13.850
So what does one plus two plus four plus
eight, and so on forever, add up to?

00:34:13.850 --> 00:34:15.850
It has no sum at all.

00:34:15.850 --> 00:34:18.570
The partial sums grow without limit.

00:34:18.570 --> 00:34:22.085
The continued function equals
minus one at two,

00:34:22.085 --> 00:34:26.670
but that is a value of the function,
not a sum of the series.

00:34:26.670 --> 00:34:28.630
Continuation extends the function.

00:34:28.630 --> 00:34:31.970
It never makes a divergent series
converge.

00:34:31.970 --> 00:34:36.068
The same goes for the famous claim
that one plus two plus three,

00:34:36.068 --> 00:34:38.410
and so on, equals minus one twelfth.

00:34:38.410 --> 00:34:43.050
Minus one twelfth is the value of
the continued zeta function at minus one.

00:34:43.050 --> 00:34:45.530
The sum itself diverges.

00:34:46.133 --> 00:34:47.353
A second limit.

00:34:47.353 --> 00:34:50.133
Continuation can depend on the path.

00:34:50.133 --> 00:34:53.573
Start with the square root near one, where
it is one.

00:34:53.573 --> 00:34:56.433
Carry it continuously once around zero.

00:34:56.433 --> 00:34:59.293
When you come back,
it has become minus one.

00:34:59.293 --> 00:35:03.753
The logarithm does the same,
gaining two pi i each time around.

00:35:03.753 --> 00:35:05.733
That story has its own video.

00:35:06.333 --> 00:35:08.393
A third limit.

00:35:08.393 --> 00:35:10.873
Sometimes there is no way across at all.

00:35:10.873 --> 00:35:15.876
This series adds z, z squared,
z to the fourth, z to the eighth,

00:35:15.876 --> 00:35:18.813
and on, doubling the power each time.

00:35:18.813 --> 00:35:21.325
It converges inside the unit circle,

00:35:21.325 --> 00:35:25.093
but near a dense set of points
on the edge it blows up.

00:35:25.093 --> 00:35:27.853
No continuation can cross the circle
anywhere.

00:35:28.467 --> 00:35:32.987
When a continuation does exist, a formula
usually does the work.

00:35:32.987 --> 00:35:37.287
Gamma's integral only works to the right
of the vertical line through zero.

00:35:37.287 --> 00:35:41.887
But gamma of z equals gamma of z plus one,
divided by z.

00:35:41.887 --> 00:35:45.407
Use that to step left,
one strip at a time,

00:35:45.407 --> 00:35:51.107
and each step adds a new pole,
at zero, minus one, minus two, and on.

00:35:55.317 --> 00:35:57.477
Back to the bump on the real line.

00:35:57.477 --> 00:36:00.726
Its recipe stopped at one
because of two points

00:36:00.726 --> 00:36:04.117
we could not see from the line,
at i and minus i.

00:36:05.117 --> 00:36:07.977
Everything since came from one demand.

00:36:07.977 --> 00:36:12.097
The complex derivative must be the same
from every direction.

00:36:12.097 --> 00:36:15.084
On an open region,
away from critical points,

00:36:15.084 --> 00:36:19.157
that makes every small piece of the map
a stretch and a turn.

00:36:19.157 --> 00:36:22.197
It lets boundary values decide the inside.

00:36:22.197 --> 00:36:24.871
It lets a loop count zeros and poles,

00:36:24.871 --> 00:36:28.557
and it turns a real integral
into a sum of residues.

00:36:28.557 --> 00:36:32.340
It allows at most one extension
to a given connected region,

00:36:32.340 --> 00:36:37.277
though there may be none, and along
different paths a continuation can branch.

00:36:38.483 --> 00:36:40.643
One question to take with you.

00:36:40.643 --> 00:36:44.043
Centre the bump's recipe
at x equals three.

00:36:44.043 --> 00:36:52.467
How far will it reach?

00:36:52.467 --> 00:36:57.027
To the poles at i and minus i, which are
the square root of ten away.

00:36:57.027 --> 00:37:00.147
About three point one six in each
direction.

